  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 AM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nA very stable/dry weather pattern will linger through the end of the \nweek under a partly to mostly cloudy sky with temperatures above \nnormal in the upper 70s and lows in the 50s.\n\nA more unsettled pattern is still on track for the weekend mainly \nfor Saturday night into the day Sunday as rain chances have \nincreased (50-70%) across the Quad State area. This will be the \nresult of increasing moisture and an ill-defined/weakening low \npressure system passing through the area. The rainfall total \nforecast has also increase across most of the area, especially west \nKY into southwest IN as tropical moisture pushes into the area from \nthe southwest. In fact, there is now about a 60% chance of seeing a \na half inch of rain or more across the aforementioned areas. Timing \nstill looks very similar in keeping most of the Halloween evening \ndry before rain arrives around or just after 8pm from the west.\n\nAfter the weekend precipitation, yet another/stronger upper level-\nlow and surface low/cold front are expected to approach/move through \nthe Quad State Monday night through at least Wednesday. The added \nprecipitation and cloud cover will allow temperatures to trend near \nnormal or cooler than normal. The latest ensemble guidance continues \nto hint at a better chance of seeing substantial rainfall with \naround a 60% chance of the Quad State seeing three-quarters of an \ninch or more of rain Monday night through Wednesday evening. There's \nstill some uncertainty on how the systems evolve next week so stay \ntuned for updates on rainfall totals! The thunderstorm threat (any \nstronger storms) will be determined by the exact track and phasing \nof the systems for early to middle of next week. It is a bit early \nto get into the details at this time.",
